INT. INSURANCE CLAIMS DEPARTMENT - NIGHT

Rows of gray cubicles stretching into dimness. A single bank of fluorescents hums over the far corner. The rest of the floor is dark, the monitors asleep. Rain streaks the windows.

A NEW HIRE (20s, brand-new lanyard, stiff shirt, a cardboard box of personal items still taped shut) stands at a cubicle entrance, reading a laminated card.

BERNADETTE (50s, cardigan, headset around her neck, the posture of someone who has sat in the same chair for twenty-five years) rolls out of the next cubicle on her wheeled chair, startling him.

BERNADETTE
You're the new one.

NEW HIRE
Yes, ma'am. I start tomorrow. They said to come and see the desk.

BERNADETTE
It's eleven o'clock at night.

NEW HIRE
I couldn't sleep.

BERNADETTE
Nobody does, the first week.

She turns on a small desk lamp. The pool of light shows a photograph of two children pinned to the cubicle wall, edges curled and sun-bleached. The New Hire sets his box on the desk.

NEW HIRE
Is this mine?

BERNADETTE
It was Harold's.

NEW HIRE
What happened to Harold?

BERNADETTE
He retired.

NEW HIRE
That's nice.

BERNADETTE
He didn't mean to.

The New Hire's smile falters. Bernadette picks at the arm of her chair.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
What's your name, dear?

NEW HIRE
Felix.

BERNADETTE
Felix. That's a good name. Lucky.

FELIX
I've never felt lucky.

BERNADETTE
(a thin smile)
That's how you know it's working.

She rolls to the window. Looks at the rain. Behind her, Felix opens the box. He takes out a small cactus in a clay pot, sets it on the desk. Then a framed diploma. He hangs it, crookedly, on the cubicle wall.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
Do you know what we do here?

FELIX
Claims. Processing claims.

BERNADETTE
We say no, Felix. That's what we do. We find the reason, and we say no.

FELIX
They told me we help people.

BERNADETTE
We help people feel they've been heard.

Felix straightens the diploma. It tilts again. He leaves it. He sits in the chair and gives it a small experimental spin.

FELIX
Is it hard?

BERNADETTE
The first year, yes. After that you get a callus.

FELIX
On what?

BERNADETTE
Wherever it hurts.

A long pause. The rain eases. Felix looks at the photograph of the two children.

FELIX
Is that Harold's family?

BERNADETTE
It's mine.

FELIX
Oh. I'm sorry. I thought...

BERNADETTE
It's all right. They're grown. They don't call.

She wheels back toward her cubicle, stops at the entrance, and looks over her shoulder.

BERNADETTE (CONT'D)
Felix. If you decide to leave, don't wait twenty-five years. Leave in the first week.

FELIX
Is that advice?

BERNADETTE
It's a courtesy.

She goes. Felix sits in the dim lamplight, the cactus at his elbow, the diploma crooked above him. In the next cubicle, a kettle clicks on. He doesn't move for a long time.
